## 5. Architecture and local runtime

Inspect the repository and existing site/export before making a final stack decision. The earlier roadmap proposes a Next.js + TypeScript frontend, a server-side integration layer, and WordPress/WooCommerce as the authoritative content and commerce backend. This remains the preferred starting point when supported by actual project constraints. It is a proposal, not a verified statement about all existing backend configuration.

Use one content authority and one commerce authority. Do not create independent order, stock, user, or payment stores merely to support a new UI. With the baseline architecture, retain WordPress-supported MySQL/MariaDB storage and WooCommerce-managed order storage. Use supported APIs and engine interfaces, including compatibility with the configured WooCommerce order storage mode; do not assume a particular order-table structure. Do not add PostgreSQL, Redis, or a separate auth service without a demonstrated need.

The frontend should use supported stable framework and runtime versions, strict TypeScript, server-rendered or statically generated public content where appropriate, and small interactive client components. Select compatible package versions from current official documentation at implementation time. Commit exact dependency lockfiles and document runtime versions. Avoid pre-release dependencies and abandoned plugins.

Keep content, commerce, auth, requests, and media behind typed adapters. Prefer a server-side backend-for-frontend for secret-bearing operations. Never send privileged WooCommerce REST credentials, WordPress application passwords, webhook secrets, or mail keys to the browser. Use minimal explicit data transfer objects. Validate every mutation at the server boundary. In Next.js, treat route handlers and server actions as independently exposed endpoints with explicit authentication and authorisation.

### 5.1 Commerce compatibility gate

Before choosing a headless checkout, run a local/sandbox compatibility spike with the selected gateway, variations, shipping, tax, stock, guest/customer sessions, authentication, and return flow. Use supported Store API mechanisms and documented session/token handling. A cart token is sensitive session capability data; it is not an administrator credential.

If the gateway or account bridge does not work reliably headlessly, use a coherently redesigned native WooCommerce cart, checkout, and account boundary. Keep these screens visually consistent with the public site. Do not send a headless cart token to a native checkout and assume its cookie session exists. Prove cross-origin/subdomain cookie behaviour and login/cart transfer, or keep the relevant flows together on a verified same-origin path. Record the choice in an architecture decision record.

If there is no existing backend export, build a local CMS/commerce sandbox with reversible, labelled seed data. Do not connect to a live database by default. Replacing WordPress/WooCommerce requires a separate documented migration and operational decision; do not default to an improvised payment/order engine.

### 5.2 Local environment

Provide one documented setup path for the owner's Windows + VS Code environment, with Docker Desktop or a supported alternative for the backend. Bind development services to loopback by default. Supply `.env.example` with names and explanations only, seed/import scripts, database migrations for owned tables, persistent local volumes, and a local mail catcher. Ensure a clean checkout can start using the README.

Keep development, staging, and production credentials, databases, media, webhook endpoints, mail destinations, and analytics separate. Test mode must be visible in admin. No live charge, production email blast, live form submission, or production data overwrite may occur during tests.

