### 6.2 Responsive and accessibility rules

Target WCAG 2.2 AA and record an actual conformance review, including manual checks. Do not label a page compliant based only on an automated score. Use semantic HTML, meaningful headings, labels and error associations, keyboard-complete navigation, visible focus, accessible names, and appropriate alternative text. Respect zoom, reflow, contrast and target size requirements. Custom dialogs must manage focus correctly and return it on close. Reserve space for images and controls.

Check at 360, 390, 768, 1440, and 1920 px, with a 320 CSS px reflow check and 200% text zoom. Avoid accidental horizontal scrolling. Product tables can have a labelled, accessible scroll container. Test screen reader output for the main form, navigation, checkout, cart updates and validation errors. Provide accessible alternatives for essential video information.

## 7. Motion and the 1920 × 1080 hero

The source requirement is landscape 1920 × 1080, aspect ratio 16:9. Use a reserved `aspect-ratio: 16 / 9` wrapper and `object-fit: contain`. Do not default to a viewport-height hero or crop it into a portrait frame. A smaller mobile rendition may change resolution while keeping the complete composition and ratio. Never stretch or conceal the video to imitate an unrelated reference.

Support a public web rendition, poster, optional alternative renditions, and captions/transcript where the content requires them. Use muted inline autoplay only where permitted and appropriate; always handle autoplay failure. Show the poster on missing/failed video, reduced motion, or data-saving policy. Provide keyboard-accessible play/pause controls for automatic looping motion. Do not make access to site content depend on the video loading or playing. Pause unnecessary offscreen/background playback.

Separate the pre-rendered product animation from interface motion. Codex can implement transitions and playback. It cannot reconstruct an accurate product animation from a reference link without the required media or 3D assets.

Proposed motion tokens, to be tuned after visual inspection:

| Use | Initial range | Constraint |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Button and control feedback | 120–180 ms | Immediate response; no layout jump |
| Card/focus transition | 180–240 ms | Subtle transform/opacity |
| Section reveal | 350–600 ms | Small displacement; content visible if script fails |
| Group stagger | 40–80 ms | Short bounded sequences |
| Drawer/modal | 200–320 ms | Focus and escape behaviour work during transitions |
| Reduced motion | Immediate or short fade | No parallax, long travel, or forced autoplay |

Use CSS for simple transitions and one established motion library if justified. Avoid multiple animation stacks. Animate transform/opacity where practical, clean up observers/listeners, and avoid per-frame React state for decoration. Do not hijack scroll, block back navigation, require a custom cursor, or introduce a long preloader. Any deliberate scroll-driven scene must degrade to usable static content and meet performance/accessibility budgets.

Admin exposes approved presets and bounded intensity/duration options, not raw JavaScript, arbitrary HTML, unrestricted CSS, or user-authored animation code. Changing an animation preset must not change the underlying information architecture.

