## 11. Commerce, checkout, and accounts

### 11.1 Catalogue, variations and cart

Only offer purchase for confirmed purchasable products. For variable products, require a valid product/variation combination server-side and obtain its own stock and price. Recompute totals on the server, including discounts, tax, shipping and quantity rules. Client-submitted prices, totals, stock or success flags are never authoritative.

Test persistence, quantity update/removal, session expiry, stock changes, unavailable variants, invalid quantities and two independent browsers/users. After login, prove the selected engine's cart merge/restore behaviour. Exclude cart, checkout, private API responses and account pages from shared CDN/framework caching. Do not leak cart/session identifiers through URLs, logs or analytics.

### 11.2 Orders and payment

Use a supported gateway and provider-hosted fields/redirect checkout as appropriate. Never store or log raw card numbers or security codes. Confirm actual PCI obligations with the merchant/provider; using a hosted gateway is not itself a certification of this implementation.

Create and transition orders through the commerce engine with server-generated totals. Use durable idempotency controls, atomic state transitions and engine-supported stock reservation/release. Test the last unit under concurrent checkout, duplicate clicks, expired sessions, failed/cancelled/pending payments, asynchronous completion, retries and refunds. Do not mark an order paid because the browser returned to a success URL.

Verify gateway callbacks according to the selected provider's current documentation. When the provider requires raw-body signature verification, verify before parsing/mutating. Validate event freshness/context, account/environment, amount, currency and order references. Deduplicate using persisted provider event identifiers and support safe retries/out-of-order delivery. Persist receipt/enqueue durably before acknowledging where processing is asynchronous. Send exactly the appropriate order email/stock action once per valid transition. Provide reconciliation for pending or ambiguous payments.

Expose order confirmation and guest order lookup only through a documented engine-authorised session/token mechanism. An incrementing order ID is insufficient. Restrict refund actions and audit them; never test a real refund/charge without scope-specific authorisation.

### 11.3 Accounts

Use the selected engine's maintained authentication and password hashing. Registration/guest checkout policy must be confirmed. Do not create a weak parallel identity store. Use generic recovery responses, expiring single-use tokens, throttling and session invalidation where appropriate. Provide own-profile, address and order views with server ownership checks. Define logout, session expiry, account changes and customer-data handling. Verify customer A cannot read or mutate customer B through page URLs or APIs.

## 12. Enquiries, warranty, and service workflows

