## 14. Security implementation and verification

Use OWASP ASVS 5.0.0 Level 2 as the target for applicable application controls, with a requirement-by-requirement applicability/evidence matrix. Confirm the current stable standard when implementing and record the version used. ASVS is a verification framework; a checklist does not prove certification or complete security. The controls below are project requirements and must be tested against actual architecture, CMS/plugins and infrastructure.

### 14.1 Threat model and access

Document trust boundaries for browser, frontend/server, CMS, commerce, gateway, mail, external calculator, worker and public/private storage. Consider customer data theft, forged paid orders, stock/price tampering, upload abuse, draft/preview leakage, role escalation and malicious imports. Track each finding with severity, affected route, evidence, fix and retest. Deny access by default and test both horizontal ownership and vertical role boundaries. Restrict sensitive exports and bulk actions.

### 14.2 Authentication and sessions

Use maintained engine authentication. Require staff MFA before production administration through a supported solution, with recovery and session revocation. Do not build ad hoc cryptography. Configure secure production cookies with HttpOnly where appropriate, appropriate SameSite/path/domain scope, TLS and session lifetime. Rotate/revoke sessions at relevant identity changes. Do not store privileged bearer tokens in browser local storage. Bound login/reset attempts using shared storage at the actual deployment scale, without allowing trivial permanent account lockout attacks. Never print bootstrap passwords in shared logs.

### 14.3 Request protection and validation

Check authentication, capability and object ownership inside every server mutation. Use engine anti-CSRF protections for cookie-authenticated operations with proper token and origin handling. SameSite cookies alone are not the complete defence. CORS must allow only the required origins; do not combine wildcard origins with credentials. Never mutate state through GET requests. Validate data types, lengths, formats and allowed values. Use parameterised queries/engine-safe database APIs, contextual output escaping and maintained rich-text sanitisation. Do not render untrusted raw HTML or use user strings as executable templates, SQL, shell commands or file paths.

### 14.4 Files and storage

Verify type using extension, MIME and file signature/decoder checks; no single check is sufficient. Generate storage names, prevent traversal, cap file size/dimensions/count and protect upload completion. Quarantine and scan according to the selected production upload policy before releasing files. Apply least-privilege storage/worker permissions, execution prohibition and safe content-disposition. Private evidence must never land in a public WordPress uploads directory without an independently enforced private delivery design. Authorise each retrieval; scope short-lived download links and avoid logging them. Test direct unauthenticated object URLs, stale links, MIME spoofing and oversized files.

### 14.5 External URLs and server-side fetching

Prefer owner-uploaded assets. Storing a design reference URL does not authorise fetching arbitrary URLs server-side. If imports fetch media, restrict schemes and approved domains, block credentials and private/loopback/link-local/metadata destinations for both IPv4/IPv6, validate DNS and every redirect, cap response size/time, and use restricted egress. Do not follow arbitrary redirects through an image optimiser or proxy. Prevent open redirects in navigation, callback, login-return and SEO redirect settings. Treat external calculator/embed origins as explicit allowlist entries, not free-form script injection.

### 14.6 Secrets and platform hardening

Keep secrets in environment configuration or an approved secret store outside the repository and client bundle. Separate environment keys and minimise scopes. Admin may show connection status/masked identifiers; never display stored secrets to editors. Disable dashboard code editing and unneeded CMS endpoints/features after compatibility review. Maintain WordPress core/plugins/themes and minimise the installed set. Add secret scanning, dependency/plugin inventory and vulnerability review with recorded versions. Remove unsupported/high-risk components rather than hiding audit output. Do not use forced upgrades without compatibility tests.

Configure TLS, content type protection, a deliberate referrer policy, frame embedding policy and a tested Content Security Policy. Introduce CSP in report-only mode before enforcement where needed. Allow only required payment/media/embed origins; avoid blanket `unsafe-eval` or permissive wildcard exceptions. Apply HSTS only after HTTPS/subdomain readiness. Admin and private content require suitable cache-control and frame protection; security headers must not break legitimate payment flows.

### 14.7 Abuse, jobs and logging

Apply route-specific rate/body/time limits to authentication, forms, upload initiation, checkout, search and admin exports. Ensure they work across instances and respect only configured trusted proxy headers. Put limits on worker concurrency, retries and queue age. Verify the gateway before exempting its callback from ordinary anti-CSRF handling; do not open unrelated endpoints.

Use structured redacted logs with correlation IDs for failed logins, denied access, relevant staff changes, import/publish actions, upload processing and payment transitions. Do not log passwords, card details, session/cart/reset/preview tokens, complete request bodies or private attachment URLs. Protect audit log access and retention. Ordinary staff must not be able to rewrite their history through admin. Track operational errors without exposing customer data to public diagnostics.

### 14.8 Privacy and retention

Inventory personal data, processors and access paths. Implement the company-approved retention/deletion/export workflow, separating marketing preferences from operational records. Respect required order/audit retention before deleting transactional records. Use synthetic or minimised data in staging and encrypted backup storage with restricted access. Load nonessential analytics/marketing scripts only according to the approved consent policy. Do not claim legal compliance based on generic generated text; obtain approval of the actual notices and business rules before release.

No unresolved exploitable Critical/High security finding is acceptable at release. Record lower-severity risks and their disposition. Any acceptance of residual business risk must be explicit and must not relabel an unimplemented critical control as passed. Perform active security testing only against authorised local/staging targets; do not run intrusive scans against the live reference site.

## 15. Performance and reliability

Set a measured baseline and record device, browser, network, build and test conditions. The target for real-user Core Web Vitals is LCP ≤ 2.5 s, INP ≤ 200 ms and CLS ≤ 0.1 at the 75th percentile, evaluated separately for mobile and desktop. Before real traffic exists, report laboratory proxies and their limitations; never call them field results.

Initial engineering budgets, which are project targets rather than external standards: aim for no more than 250 KB compressed initial application JavaScript for public brochure templates, excluding explicitly measured third-party integrations; target a hero poster below 250 KB and a web hero rendition below 15 MB where visual quality permits. Record actual transferred sizes, exceptions and fixes. Do not preload the entire video or let it delay primary content merely to hit a visual effect. Optimise first-frame/poster delivery and page rendering.

Use responsive AVIF/WebP where appropriate with compatible fallback, known image dimensions, font subsetting/limited weights and deliberate loading. Lazy-load offscreen media and optional motion. Cache only suitable public responses with defined invalidation. Avoid N+1 content requests and unbounded queries. Check real CDN and framework cache headers with two users. No private page or response may be cached publicly for performance.

Handle backend/media/payment-provider outages with useful states, controlled timeouts and safe retries. Never turn an unknown payment response into a successful order or a failed mail send into lost customer data. Add health checks that expose only minimal status, readiness for dependent services, bounded job retries, queue failure visibility, and disk/storage monitoring.

