## 18. Verification and release gates

Run checks appropriate to the selected architecture: strict type checking, lint/static analysis, production frontend build, plugin/backend checks, schema/migration verification, meaningful unit/integration tests and browser flows. Verify current supported commands rather than blindly copying an obsolete framework command. Use a database/test store isolated from production.

| Area | Required evidence |
| --- | --- |
| Route coverage | All 44 known legacy decisions plus newly discovered routes; expected status, correct content/template and no broken internal navigation |
| Design | Screenshots of homepage, catalogue, product, service, form, cart, checkout, account and custom admin at relevant target widths |
| Hero/motion | 16:9 frame, poster, failure/autoplay-denied path, pause, reduced-motion, mobile performance and no layout jump |
| Admin | Ten owner scenarios; persistence, draft isolation, preview scope, publish invalidation, revision restore, permission denials |
| Commerce | Simple/variable products, totals, stock, shipping/tax, cart persistence, concurrent last-unit test and independent customer sessions |
| Gateway | Sandbox success/failure/cancel/pending, invalid callback, duplicates, retries/out-of-order events and no duplicate order/email/stock action |
| Accounts | Login/logout/recovery/expiry, safe recovery response, valid token lifetime and two-customer isolation |
| Forms | Server validation, durable record, duplicate handling, mail failure/retry, appropriate state transitions and safe context |
| Files | Oversize/type spoofing denial, private object/URL access denial, expired links, processor failure and approved public publication |
| Security | ASVS applicability/evidence matrix, dependency/plugin/secrets review, ownership/role/CSRF/XSS/upload/SSRF/cache tests and retested fixes |
| Accessibility | Automated scan plus keyboard, focus, zoom/reflow, contrast, screen reader and reduced-motion checks |
| Performance | Reproducible production-build lab report, payload budgets, before/after evidence and field-monitoring plan |
| Migration/operations | Dry-run import, counts/references reconciled, redirects/canonicals, tested restore, cutover/rollback and monitoring |

Test Chrome, Edge, Firefox and WebKit/Safari coverage where available, plus an actual mobile browser when available. Clearly identify browser-engine emulation versus physical-device testing. Do not claim checks that could not run. Fix material console errors, failing requests, broken media and hydration problems. Save browser traces/screenshots for failures and representative visual acceptance; avoid capturing real private customer data.

Prefer focused tests of business/security boundaries over tests that merely duplicate implementation details. Authentication, amounts, variants, ownership, uploads, publication state and webhook idempotency require automated regression coverage. Simple copy/style changes can be verified visually without unnecessary test expansion.

Release gates: content/business approval; complete required route/workflow coverage; proven commerce/gateway boundary; passing admin editing scenarios; no open exploitable Critical/High findings; accessibility/performance evidence; production secret/environment readiness; tested backup/restore and rollback; and explicitly authorised cutover. Unavailable required integrations remain blockers or require an explicit scope decision.

